course: dictionary
lesson: 7002
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. Paralyse has three: to make someone lose the power to move, to stop something working, and to be too frightened to move. «Four friends lowered a paralysed man through a roof to Jesus.» The man cannot move his body, so paralysed means unable to move.
explain: Read the words around the word and ask which meaning fits. «Snow can paralyse a city» has no person in it, so it cannot mean a body losing movement. Here it means the city stops working as it normally does. Then swap your meaning into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
explain: Stories often use paralysed to describe feelings. «He stood paralysed with fear as the dog ran at him» means he was so scared he could not move or act. The words «with fear» are the clue that this is about a feeling, not an injury.
fact: Paralyse is a verb, so its forms are paralyses, paralysed and paralysing.
fact: To paralyse someone means to make them lose the ability to move part or all of their body, through injury or illness.
fact: To paralyse something means to stop it working or moving normally.
fact: Some spiders and snakes paralyse their prey with venom.
fact: Hitting rock, or hitting water from high up, can break the back or neck, and tombstoning has paralysed people in the UK.
exercise: «Four friends lowered a paralysed man through a roof to Jesus.» What does paralysed mean here? | Very happy to see his friends | Lying down to sleep | *Unable to move part or all of the body | Covered in paint
exercise: «Snow can paralyse a city.» What does paralyse mean? | Make the city bigger | Paint the city white | *Stop the city working or moving normally | Make the city very beautiful
exercise: «He stood paralysed with fear as the dog ran at him.» What does paralysed with fear mean? | Happy and laughing loudly | Ready to race the dog | *So frightened he could not move or act | Very tired after a long walk
test: Which is the -ing form of paralyse? | *Paralysing | Parallel | Paralyst | Paralysis
test: «Tombstoning has paralysed people in the UK.» What does paralysed mean here? | Made to laugh | *Made unable to move part of the body | Taught to swim | Made very rich
exam: «A spider can paralyse its prey with venom.» What does paralyse mean here? | To make the prey invisible | To make the prey hungry | *To make the prey unable to move | To make the prey very happy
